WEBVTT

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:29.420
<b>LEGEND</b>

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:29.420
<b>OF THE</b>

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:29.420
<b>  GALACTIC HEROES</b>

00:01:53.900 --> 00:02:03.280
Chapter IV

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.790
On October 4th,
Space Year 788...

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:14.490
...Yang Wenli was about to visit the
residence of Admiral Alfred Rosas.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.900
"October is a
country of sunset."

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.650
"Humans and light walk in
the sunset without a sound."

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.860
Whose poem was it ?

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.670
It suits a landscape
like this very much.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:29.360
Huh ?

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:37.970
I wish I were walking here with a
lover, but this is work, unfortunately.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.590
Come to think of it, I wonder
what Jessica is doing now.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.070
I think Jessica likes Lap.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:50.940
She's never said so out loud, but as
dense as I am, even I can sense it.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.740
Oh no, I have to
get back to work.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.000
Let's see...

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:01.490
Maple Hill, 17th Street.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.210
It's not far.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.940
I think the greeting ought
to be "How do you do ?"

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:23.480
Ah, eh, um, well...

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.880
You must be
Lt. Commander Yang Wenli.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.030
Um...yes.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:29.790
I heard about this
from my grandfather.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:30.690
Please come in.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:35.500
T...thank you.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.890
She's his granddaughter.

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:37.690
Whaddya know...

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.480
Please wait here.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:42.840
Um...thank you.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.180
This is nice.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:51.930
A room like
this is ideal.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:58.760
But a mansion with a room like this probably
isn't appropriate for you until you get old.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.520
In my case, it'll probably
take another thirty years.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.610
It's an honor to have you
visit me, Lt. Commander Yang.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:23.150
As reclusive as I am, I know
the name of the Hero of El Facil.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.480
Um...I'm flattered.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:37.300
Well, I heard you wanted to
know more about Bruce Ashbey.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.410
Yes, actually...

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:45.390
I see.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:48.030
Thank you,
I appreciate it.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:51.050
Since my wife passed
away, I've lived alone.

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.410
So something like this isn't
much of a problem for me.

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:56.670
I see...

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:00.830
This might be
a bit too strong.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:04.880
Yes, indeed.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:09.320
It's been more than forty
years since Ashbey died.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.040
I probably shouldn't rush him.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:25.360
Among Ashbey's staff, there were so many who were more competent than I am.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:33.390
It's only because I lived longer than them that I'm impudent enough to be their mouthpiece.

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:42.480
March Jasper died.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:45.250
Baron Warwick died.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:51.040
Fang Tchew-ling,
Bertini, and Cope, too.

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:54.080
Everyone's gone now.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:04.390
We used to say
this among ourselves.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:10.680
If some of us live longer than the others, we'll never know what kind of nasty stuff they might say...

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:14.470
...so we had to live longer
than the others, by all means.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:22.200
As the saying goes,
the good die young.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:33.710
Sir, about the strange rumor
involving Fleet Admiral Ashbey...

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:41.790
When there is a legend,
there's always a counter-legend.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:52.490
There's no reason that all of Ashbey's contemporaries had to love, admire, and understand him.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:56.040
The letter in question...

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:01.490
...claims that Admiral Ashbey's death was murder and not a result of battle.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:10.370
The military office can't ignore such a
dishonorable rumor involving Ashbey's death.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:15.850
I see, that's why you came all
this way to see this old man.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:20.230
Sir, is there anything
you can think of ?

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:21.570
No.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.570
Even if there were,
I wouldn't say.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:28.180
I'm sorry for
your trouble, but...

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:39.800
I cooperated in
creating his legend.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:45.620
I don't think I unjustifiably glorified
him, but I don't have any desire...

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:49.410
...to destroy the image of
Ashbey inside myself, either.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:55.600
Among our contemporaries, there must have
been many who could question me then, but...

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:03.420
The dead have
no voice, right ?

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.100
That's right.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:18.330
No matter what I say here,
there's no one who can refute me.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.090
It's the victory
of the survivors.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:29.880
Just as small talk, can you please
talk about Fleet Admiral Ashbey...

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:33.620
...and the other Admirals,
as seen through your eyes ?

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:39.550
I don't know if it'll
be of any use to you.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.090
Ashbey was exceptionally good at
judging his chances in a given battle.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:48.040
I can't describe it other than
to say he was a genius at it.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:49.500
Chances in a battle...?

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:50.390
Yes.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:55.390
If you're one minute too fast or too late,
the operation will fall apart.

00:11:55.390 --> 00:12:01.580
Ashbey always took control of those
one-in-ten-thousand kinds of opportunities.

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:04.250
It was a god-like skill.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:10.570
"The goddess of time was on his side
instead of the goddess of victory."

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:13.490
Some people used to say that.

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:19.350
Personally, I think Ashbey was a
tactician, rather than a strategist...

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:25.690
...but even then, he was an incomparably
grand and graceful tactician.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.550
That assessment isn't
an overestimation.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:33.140
Ashbey's accomplishments of consecutive victories in consecutive battles are proof themselves.

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:42.490
But the issue is, what kind of germs that
indisputable gracefulness might have within itself.

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:54.670
Sir, you said Admiral Ashbey was
more of a tactician than a strategist.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.140
On a given battlefield...

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:03.670
...there was no one who demonstrated as
much god-like tactical ability as he did.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:07.230
He won each
time he fought.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:11.300
But to put it another way,
he always won the battles...

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:17.100
...but did not have much influence on
the outcome of the history of space.

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:22.140
Certainly, since the
Battle of Dagon...

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:25.610
...there've been no real changes in the balance of power between the Empire and Alliance.

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:28.320
Take, for example,
the Iserlohn Corridor.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:35.930
Before the Empire built Iserlohn Fortress, the
Alliance also had a fortress construction plan.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:43.300
To tell the truth, Ashbey
also had a part in that plan.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:49.740
But Ashbey was more interested
in leading his fleet in battle...

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:57.490
...so in exchange for enlarging his fleet in the upper house of Congress, that plan was turned down.

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:02.590
Military equipment requires
a lot of financial resources.

00:14:02.590 --> 00:14:07.960
If the fortress was going to be that expensive,
he'd rather use the money on the fleet...

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:11.200
...was Ashbey's tactical thinking.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:15.120
That in itself is characteristic
of a battlefield commander, but...

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:22.760
In the end, the Empire built
the fortress at Iserlohn first.

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:29.550
If Ashbey had pushed for his fortress construction plan harder history might have been different.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:38.480
By the way, what do you think
of Admiral Ashbey as a person ?

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.520
He had both confidence
and righteousness about him.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:53.490
Because of that, he was continuously
in conflict with his superiors.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:57.140
One time, when he was still a
Lt. Commander - the same as you...

00:14:57.140 --> 00:15:00.490
...his superior yelled at him.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:06.210
You little piece of nothing,
you're way too cocky.

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:11.000
Do you actually think those victories are all 
because of your abilities and not because of luck ?

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:14.650
You think nothing is
impossible for you ?

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:21.730
Of course there are things
that are impossible for me to do.

00:15:22.430 --> 00:15:24.140
Such as...

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:26.980
...failing as
much as you do.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:35.490
In order for you not to get angry when
someone says something like that to you...

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:38.740
...you must have an enormous amount
of lenience and confidence in yourself.

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:42.140
And there aren't many
people who have that.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:53.080
But Ashbey, with his talent and track
record, repelled his superiors' antipathies.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.490
Naturally, he had many enemies.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:01.540
Even so, that was more
than forty years ago.

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:09.740
Then, since everything has been discussed{prescribed},
I'll pry into more personal matters.

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:14.140
You were acquainted with 
Admiral Ashbey's first wife, correct ?

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:19.620
Even at the time of the divorce,
I heard that you took part in mediation.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.200
It wasn't something
I wanted to do.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:28.140
From Ashbey's perspective,
he was begging a friend for a favor...

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:31.740
...but it put a great
psychological burden on me.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:42.140
First, I humorously but
definitively said no, saying...

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:44.740
"I have no business in meddling with
matters between a man and a woman."

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:47.140
"My wife alone is
too much for me."

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:51.490
"And you've got more experience and
knowledge in that field than I'll ever have."

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:59.130
To me and the others on the staff,
Adelaide was an old friend.

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:03.700
And we all thought, "Ashbey should
refrain from having outside affairs."

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:08.140
"While Adelaide is still smiling, it might be okay,
but when she stops, things won't be pretty."

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:09.790
That was what we felt.

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:14.850
The reason for divorce was
his faithlessness, I presume ?

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:17.470
"Faithlessness," huh ?

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.130
If it were faithlessness, then
that might have been okay...

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:24.040
...but in those faithless
affairs, Ashbey was faithful.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:33.950
You're still
single, aren't you ?

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.290
Then you might
not know it...

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:42.740
...but among men, there seem to be
some who instinctively are averse...

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:45.660
...to being bound to
their family upon marriage.

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:53.030
Besides, Ashbey was what we commonly
call a women lover and family hater...

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:59.670
...and he must have felt uncomfortable being restricted and meddled with by Adelaide.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:09.690
I understood that, but in the end,
I served as a mediator in their divorce...

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:11.740
...though after many
arguments, of course.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:21.220
Adelaide was much more
composed than I expected.

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:25.790
She calmly understood that her
husband's feelings were no longer hers...

00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:27.990
...and accepted the divorce.

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:30.780
In fact,
I was surprised.

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:34.440
But you'll eventually
come back to me.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:38.230
The only place you can
come back to is my side.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:54.400
Those were Adelaide's
parting words.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:59.250
Even though Ashbey was always
fearless on the battlefield...

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:02.590
...he couldn't hide his
expression of apprehension.

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:07.800
I was afraid, too.

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:11.390
I don't want to be so blunt...

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:15.710
...but I believe it's a bad idea to ever make a woman angry.

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:19.500
That's how it is...

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:22.940
Did you sympathize
with Mrs. Adelaide ?

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:29.400
I didn't feel like
blindly siding with Ashbey.

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:31.440
He's more clever
than I am, I guess.

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:39.250
Adelaide was a strong-willed
woman and she had her faults, too.

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:42.440
But so did Ashbey - it was even.

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:45.700
Above all, she
loved her husband.

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:50.130
Our other friends
thought that, too.

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:58.130
And we felt so even more
as she moved on cleanly.

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:00.880
Afterwards, did anything
happen with the lady ?

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:02.990
No, that was
the end of it.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:06.130
I don't know
what became of her.

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:09.460
Are you suspecting she might
be the sender of the letters ?

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:12.690
No, I didn't mean it that way.

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:17.000
By the way, I heard your
wife passed away, but...

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:21.490
Yes.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:27.780
That was in July of the
same year Ashbey died.

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:32.120
Even before then, my
wife was in ill health...

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:35.580
...but since the expedition
had been scheduled, I had to go.

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:38.690
Departure was difficult...

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:43.040
...and I appeased her as if she were a
child and then headed to the battlefield.

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:47.400
When I returned, she had
already passed away.

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:54.370
I couldn't be there
for her when she died.

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:58.670
And that trauma lasted much longer
than I had ever thought possible.

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:07.900
I was devastated, and lost
all my emotional energy.

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:18.740
Jasper and Warwick, among
others, worried about me...

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:21.190
...and recommended that
I take some time off.

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:28.040
I also knew that I could not perform
my duties in that predicament...

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:31.800
...so I listened to their advice and
decided to take a leave of absence.

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:38.670
There will be a big battle against
the Empire sometime this year.

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:44.790
If you can't be on the General Headquarters staff,
the fleet and I will be adversely affected.

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:50.640
I'm sorry, but I don't
have any willpower.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:55.670
I'd be a liability if I stayed on duty, so
I ask you to let me go on leave for a while.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:01.040
Ashbey repeatedly urged
me to change my mind...

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:06.610
...but I adamantly refused,
and was finally granted a leave.

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:13.550
But in the end, I returned
after only one month.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:20.410
In order to fill the emotional void, I realized that I had to dedicate myself to my work.

00:22:21.470 --> 00:22:26.940
And three months later,
the 2nd Battle of Tiamat...

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:30.940
...Bruce Ashbey's final
battle, took place.

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:41.140
That was in December
of Space Year 745.

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:46.340
Alfred Rosas started to speak.

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:50.320
About what happened in the
2nd Battle of Tiamat 43 years ago.

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:55.740
Between the Galactic Empire
and the Free Planets Alliance...

00:22:55.740 --> 00:23:00.910
...that was one of the most famous
stages in the long history of bloodshed.

